#!/usr/bin/python3
"""gui-launch --space <agent Space | display:<screen>> [--guard-seconds 60] [--adopt-timeout 30] [--hide] [--allow-caller-placement] [--attach-exe <path> --attach-argv <needle>] [-- <open args | executable [args]>]

Launches a GUI app for an agent run without taking Tim's screen (twaldin-home: he works on Spaces 1-4),
and/or guards one that something else starts, then checks that nothing took it.
- Launch: with `open` arguments (`-n -a <app> --args …`) it runs `open -g`, which never activates the app.
  --hide adds -j, for launchers whose own window you never need (Prism): a hidden app's windows don't draw
  until it unhides. An executable runs as is.
- The tree, the processes the guard answers for (each remembered with its start time: a pid that comes to
  name another process is dropped):
  - an executable: it and its descendants;
  - `open`: the guard hands the app a random per-launch token (`open --env GUI_LAUNCH_TOKEN=…`), and every
    process whose environment carries it, with its descendants, is the launch's however late it appears. An
    instance without the token, even of the same app, never is. None within --adopt-timeout seconds (30) is a
    fault: the launch may be running unguarded. Use -n unless the app isn't running: `open` hands a running
    instance no environment. macOS hides the environment of its own (platform) binaries, so an Apple app is
    never found this way: launch its executable instead;
  - attach: --attach-exe <path> --attach-argv <needle>, always together, adds every process whose executable's
    real path is <path> and one of whose arguments contains <needle>, with its descendants. This covers a
    client that an app starts through LaunchServices (its parent is launchd, so it descends from no launch).
    Never by app name or bundle id. The word after --attach-argv is the needle even when it starts with -.
    Processes it matches that already run are adopted before anything else, so the app Tim is in at launch is
    never one of them. Without `--` and a launch, gui-launch only guards: until --guard-seconds pass or
    SIGTERM/SIGINT.
- Before it launches anything the guard takes its baseline: Tim's front app, his focused window and its Space
  (from yabai's window list), the Space his display shows, and yabai's Spaces by SkyLight id. A yabai query there
  that fails, misses its 2 s deadline or answers unreadably (a window row that does not say whether it has focus,
  a focused row without its id, pid or Space, a Space without its id), a direct read of his display (SkyLight)
  that does not show the Space yabai says it shows, or an AppKit app (below) that is not the guard's own or whose
  activation policy will not be .prohibited, launches nothing: exit 2.
- Guard, until --guard-seconds pass, the launched tree exits (without attach), or SIGTERM/SIGINT:
  gui-launch-guard (src/gui-launch-guard.swift) adopts processes as they activate or launch and in a scan
  of all processes every 150 ms. A theft window runs from a tree activation until focus is back with Tim's app,
  and 2 s after: inside it no activation and no Space change is taken for his, so an app macOS activates
  then never becomes the one focus goes back to and his expected Space stays the one before the theft, but for
  his takeover: an app outside the tree (other than the one focus goes back to) that becomes frontmost within
  100 ms of his HID input (sinceInputMs) is his choice (decision user, takeover), never reverted, and the Spaces
  his display shows from then until the tree next activates are his. A later takeover keeps the first one's cutoff:
  none of those Spaces or notifications is charged to an earlier theft. 100 ms is about twice the slowest of his
  activations in the after-dark and tab receipts (2.7-47 ms, 0.3-38.6 ms). The residual risk: any input counts
  (a mouse move, a keystroke, synthetic input from any process), so an app a tree's theft cascade activates
  within 100 ms of it passes for his, unreverted and unflagged. A tree app's activation is never his. Outside
  the windows, every app switch and Space change is his. When a tree
  process becomes frontmost before shutdown starts, the guard re-activates Tim's app at once, in the turn that decides the activation
  (it never waits for yabai; his app is checked first, as for the re-activation below); then, unless focus is back
  by then, it focuses Tim's restore-target window by id through
  yabai (the one he had focused at launch or after his last own app switch, never a window of the tree), or
  re-activates his app again if there is none or the focus fails (1 s). Before every focus his app is checked again,
  and so is the window's owner, by a 1 s query made for that focus whose answer counts only if no activation,
  Space change or new restore target came while it ran (it is asked again only while the focus is still wanted): a
  window it cannot vouch for is not focused, and the focus must still be wanted immediately before it is made. The
  re-activation checks his app again in the same main-queue turn. An owner query that runs out of time is an
  owner-query-timeout event, expected-slow and no problem only when the window was not focused, an activation of
  his app (at once, or the re-activation) ran, and a read after the revert showed his app frontmost and his display
  on the expected Space; otherwise a problem.
  The guard reads every display directly (SkyLight, which yabai reads too) at every Space notification, every 20
  ms through each theft window, around each restore, and once more as it ends: each Space the tree shows him is a
  breach the moment it is read, and if his display shows another Space at any moment of a theft window, focusing
  that window brings his Space back. SkyLight tells only the Space shown now, so a Space shown between two reads
  (an excursion of under ~20 ms, or longer if reads are late) cannot be named; instead each Space notification must
  be explained by a change the reads found since the notification before it (two reads positively naming two
  Spaces on one display; a display left out of a read or unreadable never counts as changed), and one that is not
  is a change and back unseen: in a theft window a breach (space-unseen), outside one his own, as his changes are.
  In a theft window only a change of Tim's own display explains a notification (sky-lead's decision A): one that
  coincides with another display's change is a breach too; but a notification whose interval (since the one before
  it) began at or after his takeover is judged as outside a theft window. So callers must not drive other displays
  (their Spaces) during a guarded launch: doing so produces false failures by design. This rests on an assumption
  [INFERENCE, not shown]: macOS posts at least one Space notification after each completed Space change (Apple
  documents only that it is posted "when a Spaces change occurs"). That they arrive at all is checked (GR1: guard
  04448d35 never got one): each change of any display that a read other than a notification's finds (at an
  activation, a poll, a restore, the end) must be followed by a Space notification within 0.5 s, or the check fails
  ("Space notifications are not arriving", once per run). macOS delivers them through an app's AppKit event loop,
  so the guard runs as an AppKit app that is never activated (activation policy .prohibited: no Dock icon, no menu
  bar, no windows, never frontmost; AppKit opens nothing for it, and a quit Apple Event ends it as SIGTERM does).
  An Objective-C exception, whether AppKit's loop caught it or nothing did, ends the guard at once whatever the
  defaults say, within 0.5 s whether or not anyone drains its output: an `error` event if stdout can take it at
  once (it may be missing), no summary, no teardown of its yabai helpers, and the guard exits 2, so gui-launch
  exits 2: treat that as a crash.
  The residual: a change no read finds is still unchecked, so missing notifications show only once a read finds a
  change; and [INFERENCE] a notification that comes more than 0.5 s after the change SkyLight shows fails the
  check. In a theft window, reads more than 100 ms apart, or a read that fails, are a problem. That is all it does
  to his windows. It moves
  every tree window to the target Space by id as Accessibility reports it created, focused or made main, and again
  whenever the tree activates, the active Space changes, or a tree window comes on screen (it reads the windows on
  screen every 100 ms: macOS can show an existing window again, a native tab selected again, on the Space Tim is
  viewing with no create event and no Space change). A tree window found on Tim's screen (Spaces 1-4, or the Space
  his display shows, unless that is the target) after the guard saw it elsewhere, or at the guard's end, fails the
  check, created or not. A new window's first moments there, where macOS opens it, are a move whose onTimSpaceMs
  counts from its first Accessibility sighting: past 250 ms that fails the check. At that sighting the guard reads
  the window's Spaces and display from SkyLight (firstSpace, firstDisplay, firstAt). A window yabai will not place
  is asked about every 50 ms for 3 s and moved once yabai lists it; then window-unknown until placed
  (window-located); one never placed fails the check unless SkyLight always showed it off Tim's screen, also when
  the guard ends within those 3 s.
  Helper windows (bench's ruling): "on Tim's screen" means ordered in on one of his Spaces (SkyLight) with
  WindowServer bounds larger than 2x2 px; what cannot be read counts as on it. Exempt, and only these: a window
  SkyLight puts on no Space (ordered out; not merely off screen: a window on one of Tim's Spaces that no display
  shows counts, at the end too) and one of 2x2 px or less, such as easl's 1x1 window and its ordered-out 500x500
  helpers. Each is recorded (window-exempt: id, pid, bounds, ordered state, Spaces), never moved, never a problem;
  the guard samples it every 100 ms from its first sighting, before any yabai query finishes, and judges one that
  orders in or grows past 2x2 px from the first sample that finds it counting. Both exemptions require readable
  membership and bounds and WindowServer presence; an incomplete sample counts, never exempts.
  A window yabai will not place or name the owner of, a window list
  that fails, and a tree window still off the target after its move are recorded as they happen; the tree's
  windows the final sweep cannot locate are a problem, and so is a tree window still off the target after its move
  (window-off-target). --allow-caller-placement is for a caller that moves the tree's windows itself afterwards:
  the guard still moves and records them, but a window off the target is then no problem if it is on a Space and
  off Tim's screen (Spaces 1-4, or the Space his display shows or should show, unless that is the target); on his
  screen, or on no Space, it is a problem all the same. The check names the placement declared (placement:
  "caller" or "target"). macOS still lets an app activate itself and
  opens its new windows on the Space Tim is viewing: the guard undoes both within milliseconds, it cannot prevent
  them. The guard exits when gui-launch does, even by SIGKILL. It bounds every yabai call over its exit and its
  output (2 s; a timeout fails the check, but for the owner query above and a final window list its retry reads;
  output that reaches 16 MiB is refused,
  whether or not the helper exits, and fails it), runs none on its main thread, and ends each with its process
  group (a member it cannot read counts as alive). At its end, work in flight (reverts included) gets 3 s to finish
  and its own final checks 5 s more (its final window list gets one retry: one absolute 4 s deadline for both tries
  including helper termination and reaping, each reply at most 2 s and shortened to leave cleanup time;
  one neither try reads fails the check, as does a summary with no final window list; the tries and their latency
  are the check's finalWindowList) (what does not finish fails the check, and so does a theft it can no longer
  revert), then any yabai helper still running is killed, with what it started; then, while a change a read found
  waits for its Space notification, it waits for that (at most 0.5 s, still polling); then its watch of the
  displays stops polling, and in one turn of its main queue takes its last read, is sealed and has the summary's
  records taken (a change still waiting for its notification then fails the check, however young; a Space
  notification after the seal is a problem, never dropped unrecorded).
- Check: no tree window on Spaces 1-4 or on the Space his display shows (unless it is the target; but a window the
  guard's final sweep found exempt, which the check lists as exemptWindows), none the guard
  found back on his screen, and no tree window off the target after the guard's move (but as
  --allow-caller-placement excuses); Tim's frontmost app unchanged (the app he had at launch, or the one
  he switched to himself during the guard); his display still showing its Space (or the one he switched to
  himself); no Space the tree showed his display in a theft window, each one a breach even when the guard brought
  his Space back (the check shows both), no Space notification in a theft window that no change of his display
  explains, and no switch of his that a theft came within 2 s of; the active display
  unchanged, by both yabai's focused display and AppKit's main screen; no fault, no yabai timeout but an
  expected-slow owner query, no gap or failed read of his display in a theft window, no change a read found
  without a Space notification within 0.5 s, no tree window the guard could
  not locate at its end (a final window list neither try read included), no unsettled or killed work. The guard
  gives focus back without moving the active display,
  so a theft from another display can leave it there (yabai still reported it after Superwhisper got focus back):
  that fails the check, and a click on his display clears it. A reverted activation alone does not fail it: the
  check lists the revert latencies by method (immediate re-activation, window focus, re-activation, and
  expected-slow owner queries).
stdout carries only JSON lines: `sudo` (the exact command that starts the guard), the guard's events (guard,
launched or guarding, attached, dropped, activation, restore-call, reverted, owner-query-timeout, restore-target,
restore-target-dropped, display-space, user-space, user-space-revoked, space-breach, space-unseen, space-restored,
space-unrestorable, window, window-unknown, window-located, window-exempt, window-list-failed, window-off-target,
final-window-list,
yabai-timeout,
problem, fault, guard-end), then `check`; `error` before exit 2. Each activation and reverted event carries sinceInputMs and
previousFront (the app frontmost before it: in the tree or not, for how long, quit or hidden; whether it just closed
its last window is not known): diagnostics only. The launched program's own output
goes to stderr. gui-launch never quits what it launched: the caller tears it down.
Agent shells live in launchd's Background session, where macOS reports no frontmost app and no activations,
so from there the guard runs in Tim's GUI (Aqua) session through the fixed root step
  /usr/bin/sudo -n -E -- /bin/launchctl asuser <uid> /usr/bin/sudo -n -E -u <user> -- <guard> <guard args>
(`sudo -u` back to Tim keeps yabai's per-user socket answering), with only PATH=/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin,
HOME, USER and LOGNAME (Tim's, from the password database) and LANG=C.UTF-8 in its environment. Caller values
reach only the guard's arguments, after the second `--`. The `sudo` event records that argv, the
environment's keys and the guard's real path and sha256 (from Aqua: the guard's argv, "wrapper": "none").
The guard's identity (real path, then device, inode, size, mtime and sha256 read through one O_NOFOLLOW
descriptor) is read when its argv is built and again immediately before it is started; any change is
refused. sudo then executes the path by name, so a swap after that second read is the residual: no
fexecve passes through sudo.
If sudo or the guard fails, nothing is launched: exit 2.
Exit 0 ok; 1 the check failed (a fault included); 2 usage, launch, sudo or guard error.
"""
import argparse
import hashlib
import json
import math
import os
import pwd
import shutil
import signal
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile

TIM_SPACES = {1, 2, 3, 4}
YABAI = os.environ.get('GUI_LAUNCH_YABAI', os.path.expanduser('~/Applications/Yabai.app/Contents/MacOS/yabai'))
GUARD = os.environ.get('GUI_LAUNCH_GUARD', os.path.expanduser('~/.local/bin/gui-launch-guard'))
# The root step into Tim's Aqua session: fixed programs, never looked up on the caller's PATH.
SUDO = '/usr/bin/sudo'
LAUNCHCTL = '/bin/launchctl'


def emit(event):
    print(json.dumps(event, sort_keys=True), flush=True)


def die(message, code=2):
    if code == 2:
        emit({'event': 'error', 'message': message})
    print('gui-launch: ' + message, file=sys.stderr)
    sys.exit(code)


def parse(argv):
    cut = argv.index('--') if '--' in argv else len(argv)
    own = argv[:cut]
    if cut == len(argv) and not {'-h', '--help'} & set(own) and not any(a.startswith('--attach-') for a in own):
        die('missing `--` before the launch command')
    # argparse would take a needle such as -Dsky.run=… for a flag: the word after --attach-argv is its value.
    if '--attach-argv' in own[:-1]:
        i = own.index('--attach-argv')
        own[i:i + 2] = ['--attach-argv=' + own[i + 1]]
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog='gui-launch', usage=__doc__.splitlines()[0], description='\n'.join(__doc__.splitlines()[2:]),
                                 formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter)
    ap.error = die
    ap.add_argument('--space', required=True, help='agent Space index, or display:<screen name> for that screen\'s visible Space')
    ap.add_argument('--guard-seconds', type=float, default=60.0)
    ap.add_argument('--adopt-timeout', type=float, default=30.0,
                    help='with open arguments: seconds to wait for a process carrying the launch token (> 0)')
    ap.add_argument('--hide', action='store_true', help='open -j: launch the app hidden')
    ap.add_argument('--allow-caller-placement', action='store_true',
                    help='the caller moves the tree\'s windows itself afterwards: a tree window off the target after the guard\'s '
                         'move is recorded, and no problem when it is on a Space and off Tim\'s screen')
    ap.add_argument('--attach-exe', metavar='PATH', help='with --attach-argv: also guard processes running this executable (real path) ...')
    ap.add_argument('--attach-argv', metavar='NEEDLE', help='... one of whose arguments contains NEEDLE')
    args = ap.parse_args(own)
    args.command = argv[cut + 1:]
    if (args.attach_exe is None) != (args.attach_argv is None):
        die('--attach-exe and --attach-argv go together')
    if cut < len(argv) and not args.command:
        die('nothing to launch after `--`')
    for flag, value in (('--guard-seconds', args.guard_seconds), ('--adopt-timeout', args.adopt_timeout)):
        if not (math.isfinite(value) and value > 0):
            die('%s must be positive' % flag)
    if args.attach_exe is not None:
        if not args.attach_argv:
            die('--attach-argv needs a needle')
        exe = os.path.realpath(args.attach_exe)
        if not (os.path.isfile(exe) and os.access(exe, os.X_OK)):
            die('no executable at %s' % args.attach_exe)
        args.attach_exe = exe
    return args


def plan(command, hide):
    """(argv the guard spawns, the guard's launch flag)."""
    if command[0] in ('open', '/usr/bin/open'):
        command = command[1:]
    elif not command[0].startswith('-'):
        exe = command[0] if os.sep in command[0] else shutil.which(command[0])
        if exe and os.path.isfile(exe) and os.access(exe, os.X_OK):
            return [exe] + command[1:], '--exec'
        die('%s is not an executable; `open` arguments start with a flag such as -a or -n' % command[0])
    if hide and '-j' not in command:
        command = ['-j'] + command
    own = command[:command.index('--args')] if '--args' in command else command
    for flag, value in zip(own, own[1:]):
        if flag == '-a' and (os.sep in value or value.endswith('.app')) and not os.path.isdir(value):
            die('no app at %s' % value)
    return ['/usr/bin/open', '-g'] + command, '--open'


def yabai(*args):
    try:
        done = subprocess.run([YABAI, '-m'] + list(args), capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10)
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as error:
        die('yabai %s: %s' % (' '.join(args), error))
    if done.returncode != 0:
        die('yabai %s: %s' % (' '.join(args), done.stderr.strip()[:200]))
    return json.loads(done.stdout)


def in_aqua():
    return subprocess.run([LAUNCHCTL, 'managername'], capture_output=True, text=True).stdout.strip() == 'Aqua'


def hop(guard_argv):
    """The root step into Tim's Aqua session and its environment, both fixed here: only guard_argv, which runs
    as Tim after `sudo -u` and the second `--`, carries the caller's values."""
    me = pwd.getpwuid(os.getuid())
    argv = [SUDO, '-n', '-E', '--', LAUNCHCTL, 'asuser', str(me.pw_uid), SUDO, '-n', '-E', '-u', me.pw_name, '--'] + guard_argv
    env = {
        'PATH': '/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin',  # system tools only: what the guard launches may run a #!/usr/bin/env script
        'HOME': me.pw_dir,     # the launched program's ~ (shells, scripts, tools read HOME before the password database)
        'USER': me.pw_name,    # yabai's client finds its socket as /tmp/yabai_$USER.socket: the guard's moves and queries
        'LOGNAME': me.pw_name,  # the login name, which some tools read before USER (Python's getpass.getuser)
        'LANG': 'C.UTF-8',     # a UTF-8 locale for the launched program's text (titles, paths, Java's and Python's encodings)
    }
    return argv, env


def identify(path):
    """The guard's identity: {'path': real path, 'sha256': …}, and its file's (device, inode, size, mtime), all
    read through one O_NOFOLLOW descriptor."""
    real = os.path.realpath(path)
    digest = hashlib.sha256()
    with os.fdopen(os.open(real, os.O_RDONLY | os.O_NOFOLLOW), 'rb') as f:
        for block in iter(lambda: f.read(1 << 16), b''):
            digest.update(block)
        st = os.fstat(f.fileno())
    return {'path': real, 'sha256': digest.hexdigest()}, (st.st_dev, st.st_ino, st.st_size, st.st_mtime_ns)


def tims_display(spaces):
    return next(s['display'] for s in spaces if s['index'] == 1)


def shown_space(spaces):
    """The Space Tim's display (the one holding Spaces 1-4) shows."""
    display = tims_display(spaces)
    return next((s['index'] for s in spaces if s['display'] == display and s['is-visible']), None)


def resolve_space(spec, spaces):
    if spec.startswith('display:'):
        screen = spec[len('display:'):]
        try:
            screens = json.loads(subprocess.run([GUARD, '--screens'], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10).stdout or '{}')
        except (OSError, ValueError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as error:
            die('gui-launch-guard --screens: %s' % error)
        if screen not in screens:
            die('no screen named %r (screens: %s)' % (screen, ', '.join(sorted(screens)) or 'none'))
        display = next((d for d in yabai('query', '--displays') if d['id'] == screens[screen]), None)
        if display is None:
            die('yabai lists no display with id %s (screen %r)' % (screens[screen], screen))
        visible = [s['index'] for s in spaces if s['display'] == display['index'] and s['is-visible']]
        if not visible:
            die('screen %r shows no Space' % screen)
        index = visible[0]
    else:
        try:
            index = int(spec)
        except ValueError:
            die('--space takes a Space index or display:<screen name>, not %r' % spec)
    space = next((s for s in spaces if s['index'] == index), None)
    if space is None:
        die('there is no Space %d' % index)
    if index in TIM_SPACES:
        die('Space %d is one of Tim\'s Spaces (1-4)' % index)
    # Tim views the display that holds his Spaces. (has-focus is no test: yabai can report the focus on a
    # virtual screen after an app there took and gave back focus.)
    if space['display'] == tims_display(spaces) and space['is-visible']:
        die('Space %d is the one Tim\'s display shows' % index)
    return index


def focus(spaces):
    """yabai's focused Space and its display."""
    return next(({'space': s['index'], 'display': s['display']} for s in spaces if s['has-focus']), None)


def name(app):
    return '%s (pid %s)' % (app.get('name') or app.get('bundle') or '?', app.get('pid')) if app else 'none'


def space_name(e):
    """The Space a record names: its index, or, for one yabai's map did not know, its SkyLight id."""
    if e.get('from') is not None:
        return 'Space %s' % e['from']
    return 'a Space yabai did not list (SkyLight id %s)' % e.get('spaceId', e.get('fromId'))


def space_breaches(summary):
    """Each change of Tim's display that the guard recorded as not his: each Space the tree showed him in a theft
    window (a breach even when the guard brought his Space back), each Space notification in a theft window that no
    read of any display explains (a change and back unseen: a breach it cannot name), each restore it made or could
    not make, or a switch it took for his until a theft came within 2 s of it. Any other record is reported as it
    is: it fails the check too."""
    found = []
    for e in summary.get('spaceRestores') or []:
        kind, expected = e.get('event'), e.get('expected', e.get('to'))
        if kind == 'space-breach':
            found.append('the tree moved Tim\'s display to %s: a breach (read by %s, %s ms after the theft)'
                         % (space_name(e), e.get('via'), e.get('sinceTheftMs')))
        elif kind == 'space-unseen':
            since = e.get('sinceTheftMs')
            when = ('%s ms %s the theft' % (abs(since), 'before' if since < 0 else 'after')) if isinstance(since, (int, float)) else 'in a theft window'
            others = e.get('othersChanged') or []
            found.append('Tim\'s display changed Space and back unseen: macOS reported a Space change %s that no read of his '
                         'display explains%s, so the Space it showed is unknown: a breach'
                         % (when, ' (other displays changed: %s)' % ', '.join(others) if others else ''))
        elif kind == 'space-restored' and e.get('ok') and e.get('to') == expected:
            found.append('the tree moved Tim\'s display to %s: a breach, although the guard brought Space %s back (%s ms after the theft)'
                         % (space_name(e), expected, e.get('latencyMs')))
        elif kind == 'space-restored':
            found.append('the tree moved Tim\'s display to %s and the guard did not bring Space %s back (it showed Space %s)'
                         % (space_name(e), expected, e.get('to')))
        elif kind == 'space-unrestorable':
            found.append('the tree moved Tim\'s display to %s and no window of his could bring Space %s back' % (space_name(e), expected))
        elif kind == 'user-space-revoked':
            found.append('a theft came within 2 s of Tim\'s display moving to Space %s: Space %s is expected again' % (e.get('space'), expected))
        else:
            found.append('the guard recorded an unknown change of Tim\'s display: %s' % json.dumps(e, sort_keys=True))
    return found


def revert_latencies(summary):
    """The reverts' latencies (ms from theft to Tim's app frontmost again), by how focus was given back (immediate,
    window, activate), and the owner queries that ran out of time before a revert's focus (ms from theft to the
    revert's return)."""
    by_method = {}
    for r in summary.get('reverted') or []:
        by_method.setdefault(r.get('method') or 'unknown', []).append(r.get('latencyMs'))
    for e in summary.get('ownerQueryTimeouts') or []:
        by_method.setdefault('ownerQueryTimeout', []).append(e.get('fallbackMs'))
    return by_method


def final_exempt(summary):
    """The tree windows the guard's final sweep found exempt (bench's ruling, GR1 addendum 4 note 3: ordered out, or
    2x2 px or less, read from WindowServer and SkyLight; no window of Tim's screen), by id, with why."""
    return {e.get('window'): e.get('reason') for e in summary.get('exemptWindows') or [] if e.get('final')}


def problems(summary, windows, focus_before, focus_after, shown_before, shown_after, target):
    """Why the launch took Tim's screen, or can't vouch that it didn't: tree windows on Spaces 1-4 or on the Space his
    display shows (unless that is the target), but those the guard's final sweep found exempt (final_exempt), a changed
    frontmost app, his display showing another Space or moved off his Space by the tree (even if brought back), the
    active display moved (yabai's focused display, or AppKit's main screen: the two can disagree, so both count), the
    guard's fault or its problems (yabai timeouts, work unsettled or killed at its end, tree windows back on his screen
    or off the target, a final window list it could not read), or no final window list recorded at all."""
    tree = set(summary.get('tree') or [])
    found = []
    if summary.get('fault'):
        found.append(summary['fault'])
    found.extend(summary.get('problems') or [])
    if not summary.get('finalWindowList'):
        found.append('the guard recorded no final read of the tree\'s windows: they cannot be vouched for')
    exempt = final_exempt(summary)
    for w in windows:
        if w.get('pid') not in tree or w.get('id') in exempt:
            continue
        if w.get('space') in TIM_SPACES:
            found.append('window %s of %s (pid %s, "%s") is on Space %s'
                         % (w.get('id'), w.get('app'), w.get('pid'), w.get('title', ''), w.get('space')))
        elif w.get('space') == shown_after and shown_after != target:
            found.append('window %s of %s (pid %s, "%s") is on Space %s, the one Tim\'s display shows'
                         % (w.get('id'), w.get('app'), w.get('pid'), w.get('title', ''), w.get('space')))
    front, user = summary.get('frontAtEnd'), summary.get('userFront')
    if not front:
        found.append('no app is frontmost at the end')
    elif front.get('pid') in tree:
        found.append('the launched %s is frontmost' % name(front))
    elif user and front.get('pid') != user.get('pid'):
        found.append('Tim\'s frontmost app changed from %s to %s' % (name(user), name(front)))
    # The Space he should see: the one his display showed, or the one he switched to himself during the guard.
    expected = (summary.get('timSpace') or {}).get('expected') or shown_before
    if expected and shown_after != expected:
        found.append('Tim\'s display shows Space %s, not Space %s' % (shown_after, expected))
    found.extend(space_breaches(summary))
    if focus_before and (not focus_after or focus_after['display'] != focus_before['display']):
        found.append('yabai\'s focused display moved from display %s (Space %s) to %s'
                     % (focus_before['display'], focus_before['space'],
                        'display %s (Space %s)' % (focus_after['display'], focus_after['space']) if focus_after else 'none'))
    screen_before = (summary.get('focusAtLaunch') or {}).get('mainScreen')
    screen_after = (summary.get('focusAtEnd') or {}).get('mainScreen')
    if screen_before != screen_after:
        found.append('the key window\'s screen (AppKit main screen) moved from %s to %s' % (screen_before, screen_after))
    return found


def main():
    args = parse(sys.argv[1:])
    spawn, launch = plan(args.command, args.hide) if args.command else ([], None)
    if not os.access(GUARD, os.X_OK):
        die('%s is missing: run ~/dotfiles/agents/shepherd/install.sh' % GUARD)
    spaces = yabai('query', '--spaces')
    space = resolve_space(args.space, spaces)
    focus_before, shown_before = focus(spaces), shown_space(spaces)
    fd, summary_path = tempfile.mkstemp(prefix='gui-launch-', suffix='.json')
    os.close(fd)
    try:
        try:
            guard, guard_file = identify(GUARD)
        except OSError as error:
            die('cannot read the guard: %s' % error)
        guard_argv = [guard['path'], '--space', str(space), '--guard-seconds', str(args.guard_seconds),
                      '--adopt-timeout', str(args.adopt_timeout), '--yabai', YABAI, '--summary', summary_path,
                      '--parent-pid', str(os.getpid())]
        if args.allow_caller_placement:
            guard_argv += ['--allow-caller-placement']
        if args.attach_exe is not None:
            guard_argv += ['--attach-exe', args.attach_exe, '--attach-argv', args.attach_argv]
        if launch:
            guard_argv += [launch, '--'] + spawn
        if in_aqua():
            argv, env, wrapper = guard_argv, None, 'none'
        else:
            (argv, env), wrapper = hop(guard_argv), 'launchctl asuser'
        # Its identity again, immediately before it starts: a guard that changed since it was read is refused.
        try:
            again = identify(GUARD)
        except OSError as error:
            die('cannot read the guard again before starting it: %s' % error)
        if again != (guard, guard_file):
            die('the guard changed between its check and its start (%s, sha256 %s, then %s, sha256 %s): refused'
                % (guard['path'], guard['sha256'], again[0]['path'], again[0]['sha256']))
        emit({'event': 'sudo', 'wrapper': wrapper, 'argv': argv, 'env_keys': sorted(env) if env is not None else None, 'guard': guard})
        # A new session keeps sudo from wrapping the guard in a pty; Ctrl-C reaches it through the handler below.
        try:
            process = subprocess.Popen(argv, env=env, start_new_session=True)
        except OSError as error:
            die('cannot start the guard: %s' % error)
        # The guard ends early on these (and still writes its summary); gui-launch waits for it, then checks.
        for sig in (signal.SIGTERM, signal.SIGINT):
            signal.signal(sig, lambda signum, _frame: process.send_signal(signum))
        code = process.wait()
        if code != 0:
            if env is not None and code == 1:  # the guard never exits 1: sudo or launchctl did
                die('the sudo hop failed (exit 1): no guard ran, so nothing was launched')
            die('the guard exited %d' % code)
        try:
            with open(summary_path) as f:
                summary = json.load(f)
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            die('the guard wrote no summary')
    finally:
        if os.path.exists(summary_path):
            os.unlink(summary_path)
    tree = set(summary.get('tree') or [])
    windows = [w for w in yabai('query', '--windows') if w.get('pid') in tree]
    spaces_after = yabai('query', '--spaces')
    focus_after, shown_after = focus(spaces_after), shown_space(spaces_after)
    found = problems(summary, windows, focus_before, focus_after, shown_before, shown_after, space)
    attached = summary.get('attached') or []
    emit({'event': 'check', 'ok': not found, 'problems': found, 'space': space, 'guard': guard,
          'windows': [{k: w.get(k) for k in ('id', 'pid', 'app', 'title', 'space')} for w in windows],
          'frontAtLaunch': summary.get('frontAtLaunch'), 'frontAtEnd': summary.get('frontAtEnd'),
          'focusBefore': dict(focus_before or {}, **(summary.get('focusAtLaunch') or {})),
          'focusAfter': dict(focus_after or {}, **(summary.get('focusAtEnd') or {})),
          'timSpace': {'before': shown_before, 'expected': (summary.get('timSpace') or {}).get('expected'), 'after': shown_after,
                       'breaches': summary.get('spaceRestores') or []},
          'attached': [{k: a.get(k) for k in ('pid', 'rule', 'via')} for a in attached],
          'dropped': len(summary.get('dropped') or []), 'reverted': len(summary.get('reverted') or []),
          'revertLatencyMs': revert_latencies(summary),
          'spaceRestored': len([e for e in summary.get('spaceRestores') or [] if e.get('event') == 'space-restored']),
          'spaceHistory': summary.get('spaceHistory') or [], 'ownerQueryTimeouts': summary.get('ownerQueryTimeouts') or [],
          'windowFaults': summary.get('windowFaults') or [], 'exemptWindows': summary.get('exemptWindows') or [],
          'placement': 'caller' if args.allow_caller_placement else 'target',
          'finalWindowList': summary.get('finalWindowList')})
    if found:
        die('FAILED: ' + '; '.join(found), 1)
    latencies = [r['latencyMs'] for r in summary.get('reverted') or []]
    exempt = len([w for w in windows if w.get('id') in final_exempt(summary)])
    print('gui-launch: ok: %d window(s) of the tree, none on Spaces 1-4%s; %d process(es) adopted; %d activation(s) reverted%s; frontmost %s'
          % (len(windows), ' but %d exempt (ordered out, or 2x2 px or less)' % exempt if exempt else '', len(attached),
             len(latencies), ' (max %.1f ms)' % max(latencies) if latencies else '', name(summary.get('frontAtEnd'))), file=sys.stderr)


if __name__ == '__main__':
    main()
